## 3. Conclusion
In summary, a unique magnetic vortex domain structure is firstly observed in the MOFs-derived Ni NPs. Limited by the symmetry spheres and boundary condition, reduced Ni core is confined in the graphited carbon shell, constructing the basic electromagnetic wave dissipation unit. Benefited from the magnetic-dielectric synergy effect, MOFsderived $\operatorname{Ni}@\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{V}$ microsphere exhibits outstanding EM absorption ability. The $\ensuremath{\mathrm{\RL}}_{\mathrm{min}}$ value of $\operatorname{Ni}@\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{V}$ microspheres can reach $-54.6$ dB at 2.5 mm. More important, the magnetic coupling network and vortex-domain reversal in the $\operatorname{Ni}@\operatorname{C-V}$ microsphere together contribute to the expanded efficient absorption frequency up to $5.0\mathrm{GHz}$ at only $2.0\mathrm{mm}$ . Meanwhile, core-shell $\mathsf{N i@C-V}$ units construct plentiful heterojunction Ni C interfaces and build connected electronic migration routes, which encourages the interfacial polarization and conduction loss. With unique magnetic vortex domain structure and synergy dissipation mechanism, MOF-derived functional microspheres show great application prospects in electromagnetic wave absorption filed.
## 4. Experimental section
## 4.1. Synthesis of Ni-MOFs precursors
All the chemicals used were of analytical grade without further purification and were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co, Ltd. Typically, $1.5\:\mathrm{mmol}$ nickel nitrate hexahydrate $_{(0.436\mathrm{~g)~}}$ and $1.5~\mathrm{g}$
PVP K-30 are poured into the mixed solution with $10~\mathrm{mL}$ distilled water $\mathrm{(H_{2}O)}$ , $10\ \mathrm{\mL}$ ethanol (EtOH) and $10\ \mathrm{mL}$ N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF). The mixed solution is magnetic stirred for $10~\mathrm{min}$ . Then, $0.15\mathrm{~g~}$ trimellitic acid $\mathrm{(H_{3}B T C)}$ , $0.15~\mathrm{g}$ terephthalic acid (TA), and $0.15~\mathrm{g}$ diaminoterephthalic acid (ATA) as the ligand are added into abovementioned solution, respectively. After another magnetic stirred for 10 min, the three solutions are transferred to a $50~\mathrm{mL}$ autoclave and keep at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for continuous heating for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The products are taken out and washed with ethanol and distilled water three times, respectively. The solid powders are placed in a vacuum drying oven at $60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ , and products are marked as Ni-MOFs-H, Ni-MOFs-S, and Ni-MOFs-V, respectively.
## 4.2. The synthesis of MOFs-derived Ni@C microspheres
Obtained Ni-MOFs precursors are annealed in a tube furnace. The calcination conditions are $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ in an Ar atmosphere, and the heating rate is $2\ {^{\circ}\mathbf{C}}/{\mathrm{min}}$ . Due to the different ligands, the final black MOFs-derived product magnetic $\mathsf{N i@C}$ powders are marked as ${\mathrm{Ni@C-H}},$ $\mathbf{Ni@C-S}$ , and $\mathsf{N i@C-V_{i}}$ , respectively.
## 4.3. Characterizations
The crystal structure and components of as-synthesized $\mathsf{N i@C}$ microspheres are characterized by the X-ray diffractometer (XRD, Bruker, D8-Advance, Germany). Raman data is obtained by the Ramoscope (inVia, Renishaw, United Kingdom). The magnetic prosperities and hysteresis loop of MOFs-derived $\mathsf{N i@C}$ powders are tested by vibrating sample magnetometer (VSM, Quantum Design, United States). The morphology and microstructure of the $\mathsf{N i@C}$ microspheres are examined by the scanning electron microscope (FESEM, S-4800, Japan) and transmission electron microscope (TEM, JEM-2100 F, Japan). Those $\mathsf{N i@C}$ microspheres are prepared with paraffin matrix with mass ratios 1:4. Then, mixed sample are compacted into a coaxial ring of $7.00\ \mathrm{mm}$ outer diameter and $3.04~\mathrm{{mm}}$ inner diameter. Related electromagnetic parameters were measured via a vector network analyzer (VNA, N5230C, Agilent, United States) in $2{\mathrm{-}}18\operatorname{GHz}$ range. The reflection loss (RL) values were calculated by the following formula:
$$
Z=\vert Z_{\mathrm{in}}/Z_{0}\vert=\sqrt{\mu_{\mathrm{r}}/\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}}\mathrm{tan}h\Bigg[j\Bigg(\frac{2\pi f d}{c}\Bigg)\sqrt{\mu_{\mathrm{r}}\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}}\Bigg]
$$
$$
\mathrm{RL}=20\log\left|\left(Z_{\mathrm{in}}-Z_{0}\right)/Z_{\mathrm{in}}+Z_{0}\right|
$$
where $Z_{\mathrm{in}}$ is the normalized input impedance of absorber, $Z_{0}$ is the impedance of free space, $\varepsilon_{\mathrm{r}}$ is the complex permittivity, $\mu_{\mathrm{r}}$ is the complex permeability, $\mathrm{~\bf~f~}$ is the frequency, $\boldsymbol{c}$ is the light velocity, and $d$ is the thickness of the absorber, respectively.
